You paid $4,100 in student loan interest, but the IRS caps this deduction at **$2,500 per year**. 
*   **The Good News:** This is an "above-the-line" deduction (an adjustment to income). You do **not** need to itemize to claim it. You can take the standard deduction *and* deduct $2,500 for your student loan interest. 
*   *Note:* Your joint income (approx. $101,140) is well below the 2023 phase-out limit for this deduction (which starts at $155,000 for married couples), so you qualify for the full $2,500.

### 3. Home Office Deduction
Because you have self-employment income ($15,000 from web design), you can claim the home office deduction against your business income. (You cannot claim a home office for your W-2 job).
You have two ways to calculate this:
*   **Simplified Method:** $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. (130 sq ft × $5 = **$650 deduction**).
*   **Regular Method:** Based on the percentage of your home used for business. 
