*   **Unreimbursed Expenses ($650):** If these are W-2 employee expenses, they are **no longer deductible** federally (the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act eliminated this through 2025). *Note: If these were expenses for your freelance web design business, they are fully deductible on Schedule C instead.*

**Verdict:** Your total itemized deductions would only be around $10,000–$12,000. You should absolutely take the **Standard Deduction of $27,700**, as it will lower your taxable income much more. Because you are taking the standard deduction, you won't itemize the $2,000 charity receipt on your federal return.

### 2. Student Loan Interest Deduction
You paid $4,100 in student loan interest. The IRS allows you to deduct up to **$2,500** as an "above-the-line" adjustment to income. 
*   You do *not* need to itemize to claim this. 
*   Your combined income (roughly $101,000) is well below the 2023 phase-out threshold for married couples ($155,000–$185,000), so you can claim the maximum **$2,500 deduction**.

### 3. Freelance Income & Home Office (Schedule C)
Your $15,000 web design income makes you a sole proprietor in the eyes of the IRS. You will report this income and deduct your business expenses against it.

**Home Office Deduction:**
Because you use 130 sq ft *exclusively* for business out of a 690 sq ft apartment, you qualify for this deduction. You have two ways to calculate it:
*   **Simplified Method:** 130 sq ft × $5/sq ft = **$650 deduction**.
